{{- /*
	The blocks below are the declarations Python spells in a way the documentation
	does not describe. Each claims one definition by the reference it is addressed
	by, and is rendered in that definition's place, so nothing here escapes the
	order of the page or the pass that walks it.

	A block writes by hand only what has to be written by hand: it renders the
	shape its kind shares for everything the documentation does say, and adds its
	own beside it. Spelling a declaration out in full here would freeze what the
	specification still owns, and the next release would drift past it unnoticed.

	Neither block pins anything, where every block of the Go target pins the way
	its definition travels. A pin earns its place by catching what a release moves
	and nobody notices, which is why the ones in the Go target hold facts the
	documentation decides: how many variants a union stands for, which way a thing
	travels. Nothing either block below leans on is decided that way. The two
	methods each adds go uncalled by a package that sends no file whichever way the
	union travels, and the fields they spell out by hand are fields tgen hands them
	— a fact whose only author is the person who would already be editing this file
	to change it. A pin claiming that someone must read the block again, over
	nothing, is what makes the next pin worth less than it says.

	There are two blocks and not three: the union a file travels as needs nothing
	written for it. The Go target declares an interface there, because the two ways
	of handing a file over have to be named somewhere a method can call them
	through; Python resolves a call on a union by looking at every variant, so
	naming FileID and Upload is naming everything the union can answer.
*/}}

{{- /*
	manual_fileid adds to the file id alias the two ways a file travels, which is
	what makes it an InputFile: a file Telegram already holds is named, never
	uploaded, so it stays in the body wherever it sits and the sink goes untouched
	either way.
*/}}
{{- define "manual_fileid"}}{{/*gotype: github.com/andreychh/tgen/targets/pythonv2.Alias*/}}
{{- template "alias" .}}

    def _place(self, sink: _FileSink, data: dict[str, Any], key: str) -> None:
        data[key] = self.root

    def _attach(self, sink: _FileSink) -> str:
        return self.root
{{- end}}

{{- /*
	manual_upload spells the upload object out, fields and all: what it holds is a
	stream of bytes, which the specification never names and no two targets spell
	alike.

	Two settings carry the stream, and neither is a concession to which way the
	object travels. arbitrary_types_allowed is what lets pydantic build a schema for
	typing.IO[bytes] at all, there being none it knows. SkipValidation is what keeps
	the check that schema would otherwise run from running: IO[bytes] is not a
	class, an instance check against one cannot succeed, and the reader a caller
	opened itself would be refused by it. So the stream goes unchecked because
	nothing can check it, and the sending of a file needs it unchecked to work at
	all. What that costs is a reader taken on trust, which is the bargain every
	arbitrary type strikes and not a loan against the direction.

	Its two ways of travelling are the ones the union exists for. Placed, the bytes
	go to the part the parameter's own key names and the body is left without that
	key at all — writing one would put a second value under it, which the receiver
	reads as the file id the upload was sent instead of. That is why placing is
	handed the body rather than asked for a value: it has none to give. Attached,
	the bytes go to a part the sink names, and the reference to that part is a value,
	so it is answered with.

	What is pinned is the one thing this block is blind to. It writes its two
	fields out by hand and never reads the ones the specification gives the object,
	which is sound only while there are none — and there are none because the
	pipeline says so, not because a page does. A field arriving here would be a
	field silently dropped, and the count is what turns it into a stopped
	generation instead.
*/}}
{{- define "manual_upload"}}{{/*gotype: github.com/andreychh/tgen/targets/pythonv2.Object*/}}

class {{.Name}}(BaseModel):
    {{.Doc}}

    model_config = ConfigDict(arbitrary_types_allowed=True)

    reader: SkipValidation[IO[bytes]]
    """The stream the bytes are read from, taken unchecked: IO[bytes] is not a
    class, so the instance check arbitrary_types_allowed would otherwise run
    against it cannot succeed, and would refuse every reader a caller opens.
    """

    name: str = ""
    """The name the file is sent under, defaulting to file when left unset."""

    def _place(self, sink: _FileSink, data: dict[str, Any], key: str) -> None:
        sink.file(key, self._name(), self.reader)

    def _attach(self, sink: _FileSink) -> str:
        return f"attach://{sink.reserve(self._name(), self.reader)}"

    def _name(self) -> str:
        return self.name or "file"
{{- end}}
